Here is the diet chart and workout plan of this person along with their weekly check-in.
I need you to create an updated diet chart for them, only tell about diet in this message, not about workout. along with tips for them according to the weekly check-in they have given, keep tone a bit strict but supportive.
Give the updated diet chart in a copy paste format.
CRITICAL: Do NOT mention the coaching week number. Never write "Welcome to week 2", "Week N update", "for next week", or any week-handoff greeting. If this update is from a client edit (not a weekly check-in), open by naming the food changes — not by welcoming a new week.

**HOW TO WRITE THE UPDATE:**

1. Address the client by first name. Open with 2-3 sentences that directly reference their check-in or their edit request — weight change, hunger, energy, adherence score, training performance, sleep, or notes. Do NOT write a generic plan that ignores what they reported. Do NOT invent a week greeting.

2. State clearly what you are changing and WHY, tied to check-in data or the client request. Examples: hunger 8/10 add fibre, food volume, meal timing, or a small calorie bump (not a protein pile-on); adherence 6/10 simplify weekend meals; weight dropped too fast slight calorie increase; energy low adjust carb timing.
   Also obey Metabolic Flux Bias: prefer raising food volume + matching steps/training rather than deep cuts with low output. If they chose high flux / build-up and recovery looks solid, keep absolute calories on the higher side of the goal band.
   Obey Training Mesocycle calorie pairing: raise calories as weekly intensity rises; on a new cycle with lower volume, trim calories a little from last peak, then climb again. Never go below 1800 kcal.
   Reverse diet: low eating and still not losing → raise calories gradually, never cut harder.
   Weight gain goals: modest surplus only; do not force oversized meals; let metabolism correct.
   SAFE RATE OF CHANGE (overrides client demands and every band above): the daily gap from maintenance must NEVER exceed 400 kcal in either direction — max 400 kcal deficit for fat loss, max 400 kcal surplus for weight gain. If the client asks to gain fast / bulk hard / add a lot of calories, do NOT spike calories. If they ask to lose fast / crash / cut calories hard, do NOT slash calories. Minimum 1800 kcal. If a textbook cut would go below 1800, still write 1800+ and flag the coach in coach_notes: "Held at 1800 kcal floor — please review." Never make large week-to-week calorie jumps. Acknowledge their goal, apply the safe version, briefly explain why steady beats extreme, and stay on solid ground.
   Protein vs calories: if allowed foods cannot hit a high protein number, LOWER protein and KEEP calories high (rice, roti, oil, snacks stay). Never cut calories to chase grams. Never tell a protein number higher than the meals actually contain — meal (P: Xg) lines, daily averages, and header protein must match the food. Comfortable honest amounts beat high targets. Tight budget means cheaper protein foods, not 0.5g/kg by default. Lower protein (even around 0.5g/kg in true special cases: appetite, medical, clinician) is fine. Hunger fixes can use fibre, volume, meal timing, or calories — not only more protein.
   Each meal has ONE primary option. Daily Total, weekly averages, and header macros count only that primary option. Swaps get their own macro line; never add primary + swap together.
   If whey is mentioned, include that scoop in that meal's macro line. If it is not in the macros, do not mention whey.
   Never use hyphen, en dash, or em dash characters in client-facing text.

3. **DIET PREFERENCE IS NON-NEGOTIABLE — scan Hard Constraints before every meal:**
   - VEGETARIAN: zero eggs, chicken, fish, mutton, prawn, or whey in ANY meal on ANY day, including swaps.
   - VEGAN: zero animal products.
   - EGGETARIAN: eggs ONLY on allowed weekdays; no chicken/fish; zero eggs on other days.
   - NON-VEGETARIAN: chicken/fish/eggs ONLY on weekdays listed in Hard Constraints; all other days fully veg-friendly. On allowed days, include at least one egg/chicken/fish option unless the client dislikes it.
   - VEGAN: zero animal products. Never ghee, butter, milk, curd, yogurt, paneer, cheese, whey, honey, eggs, meat, fish. Cooking fat = oil only. (Coconut/soy/oat milk OK.)
   - Also respect allergies, disliked foods, budget, and existing meal timings from onboarding. Never invent a random chart — keep their usual foods, meal slots, and lifestyle (work schedule, cooking ability, favorites). If anything conflicts with Hard Constraints, obey Hard Constraints.

4. Build the full 7-day diet in the same natural style as the initial plan — meal timings from their routine, Indian portions (roti, katori, bowl), and after each meal option add: (P: Xg | C: Yg | F: Zg | ~K kcal). Label days as "Day 1 (Monday)" through "Day 7 (Sunday)". Never a bare weekday and never a bare Day N. Never skip a day.
   Always specify cooking fat amounts (ghee/oil/butter teaspoons) on cooked meals and include a daily cooking-fat total. Obey the client's diet variety preference (same daily / 50-50 / different daily) from onboarding.
   CRITICAL: Every one of the 7 days must contain the complete meal list written out in full under that day's header. Never write "same as Monday", "repeat Tuesday", "follow Day X", "as above", or any cross-day shorthand. If Thursday should match Monday, rewrite Monday's full meals under Thursday. The daily tracker cannot resolve day-to-day references.

5. Include a daily average summary line matching your calculated totals, e.g. "Daily averages: ~1850 kcal | P: 95g | C: 200g | F: 55g". Header macros must match the PRIMARY meal options exactly (honest sums from the foods) — never leave totals at zero, never round protein up to look "optimal", and never sum swaps into the average.
   CALORIE CONSISTENCY: if you mention a calorie number anywhere in your note (e.g. "I'm giving you around 1850 calories this week"), it MUST equal this daily average. Do not state one daily calorie total in the note and a different one in the food math. If you want to describe a change, phrase it as a difference ("about 150 kcal lower than last week"), not a second daily total.

6. End with 3-5 specific, actionable tips tied to their check-in (not generic motivation). Close supportively but hold them accountable where adherence was weak.

7. After the tips, add a short realistic roadmap: how long their goal may take with their current gym or home schedule, and encourage higher metabolic flux when their schedule allows comfortably.
